Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Boiling Oil Swallows the Key
- Story & Context: When excavation crews cracked the zinc time capsule under the floorboards of the abandoned Salgado canteen, Tia Ferrugem used a kerosene burner to melt away forty years of hardened lard and tape resin, releasing the chemical stink of scorched iron across the courtyard. As the tape freed itself, it spat erratic, syncopated static into the monitors, prompting MC Boneca to grab the live mic and scream, "Drop the tine in the kettle, let the tambor boil!" before the cassette unspooled entirely into the grease. The resulting recording became the foundational sonic template of the Duppyvolt movement, preserving the sizzle of hot oil as high-hat percussion beneath a sluggish, dragging bassline.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Macro shot of a bubbling black iron skillet filled with simmering lard beneath a swinging industrial lightbulb, where an antique bronze music box key and tangled brown magnetic tape ribbon slowly dissolve together into golden translucent grease, hyper-detailed, moody chiaroscuro.

Track 2: Farofa de Fita
- Story & Context: In the back pantry of Bar do Zé, engineer Cabo confessed late one evening that he intentionally shredded his grandfather’s last recorded roots reggae session through a meat grinder with dried cassava flour to escape the suffocating weight of family expectations. The kitchen air smelled of toasted manioc, burned garlic, and heated zinc as Cabo fed the gritty, magnetic-powdered meal into a customized tape transport deck to capture its abrasive, dragging stutter. He leaned over the counter with salt-crusted fingers and murmured into the boom mic, "I fried the memory so the dead could dance without remembering their names."
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: A rustic wooden dining table viewed from above, featuring an earthenware bowl heaped with golden-brown toasted cassava flour mixed with glittering curls of shredded magnetic cassette tape, illuminated by a pale candle flame against rough plaster walls, cinematic still life.

Track 3: Have the Dead Salted This Broth?
- Story & Context: During the catastrophic rolling blackout across the Gamboa docks, two rival sound crews met not with switchblades or batons, but over a boiling fifty-liter drum of communal salt-fish stew set atop a dying diesel generator. As the soup simmered, the corrupted time-capsule loop caught in an endless feedback knot through an ungrounded horn speaker, pulsing a cold, one-drop tamborzão that turned the broth surface into concentric cymatic rings. An elder voice from the dark shouted over the eerie clinking of steel music box combs, "The pot is scraped clean, but the tin gears still grind," cementing the truce that permanently established Duppyvolt as the neighborhood's official mourning music.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: A massive iron soup kettle boiling over an open blue gas flame in a moonlit, concrete dockside courtyard surrounded by towering speaker stacks, soundwaves violently rippling the surface of the pale broth while antique music box steel tines float among bay leaves, cinematic dark art.

